# PHASE 2 — BUILD A SINGLE-NAME DEVELOPMENT HARNESS

Create a clean development-only evaluation path for single-stock forecasting.

It must operate point-in-time.

For each `(cutoff, symbol)` pair, construct:

```text
features known at cutoff
market state known at cutoff
cross-sectional B3 score
forward primary-horizon return
direction target = forward return > 0
```

Predictions must be written/frozen before outcomes are read, following the existing project discipline.

No look-ahead joins.

No revised historical information unless its historical availability timestamp is known.

No same-day close information if the prediction timestamp precedes it.

Reuse existing PIT machinery wherever possible.

---

# PHASE 3 — DEFINE HARD BASELINES

Before training any sophisticated model, implement and score the following single-name baselines:

### Baseline S0

Always predict positive return.

### Baseline S1

Historical unconditional probability of positive return, estimated only from prior data.

### Baseline S2

12-1 momentum translated into directional probability using development history only.

### Baseline S3

B3 translated into directional probability using development history only.

### Baseline S4

B3 + market-state conditional calibration.

These become the incumbents.

Any ML model must beat these baselines rather than merely achieve >50% accuracy.

---

# PHASE 4 — CALIBRATION FIRST

Do NOT begin with neural networks.

Start with robust, low-capacity models.

Candidate models:

* logistic regression
* ridge regression
* isotonic calibration
* Platt-style calibration
* simple monotonic binning
* hierarchical shrinkage if necessary

For direction:

```text
P(return > 0 | B3 score, market state, validated features)
```

For magnitude:

```text
E(return | information at cutoff)
```

Regularization must be chosen only inside the development data.

If sample size does not support a model, reject it.

---

# PHASE 5 — WALK-FORWARD ONLY

All performance estimates must be walk-forward.

At cutoff `t`:

training data must contain only observations whose outcomes would genuinely have been known before `t`.

This requirement matters especially because overlapping forward-return horizons can leak outcomes.

Apply an appropriate purge / embargo based on the forecast horizon.

Never randomly shuffle time-series observations.

Never perform ordinary random K-fold cross-validation.

---

# PHASE 6 — METRICS

The primary single-name evaluation must include at minimum:

### Directional quality

* accuracy
* balanced accuracy if appropriate
* log loss
* Brier score
* ROC-AUC as secondary diagnostic

### Calibration

* reliability curve / calibration bins
* expected calibration error
* observed hit rate by probability bucket

Probability calibration is more important than raw accuracy.

A model outputting 70% must empirically win approximately 70% of the time in comparable out-of-sample cases.

### Return forecast

* MAE
* RMSE
* correlation between predicted and realised return
* sign accuracy
* predicted-return decile outcomes

### Economic discrimination

Measure performance when the model has:

* low confidence
* medium confidence
* high confidence

and when it abstains.

---

# PHASE 7 — SELECTIVE PREDICTION / ABSTENTION

This is mandatory.

The model should not make actionable calls on every stock.

Create an abstention mechanism.

Examples:

```text
BUY only if:
P(up) >= threshold
AND expected_return >= minimum edge
AND uncertainty acceptable

SELL only if:
P(up) <= inverse threshold
AND expected_return <= negative minimum edge

otherwise:
HOLD / NO EDGE
```

Do NOT tune thresholds to maximize historical profit.

Thresholds must be selected using predefined development criteria.

Report:

* coverage
* accuracy conditional on coverage
* Brier/log loss
* mean realised return
* breadth across symbols
* stability across time/regimes

We want a model that knows when it does not know.

---

# PHASE 8 — CROSS-SECTIONAL + SINGLE-NAME CONSISTENCY

The two prediction views must agree logically.

Example:

If NVDA is cross-sectional percentile 95 but its absolute expected return is negative because the entire market is in a severe risk-off state:

the correct interpretation may be:

```text
NVDA is one of the strongest stocks relatively,
but absolute expected return remains unattractive.
```

Do not force relative ranking into an absolute BUY signal.

Keep these concepts separate in the data model.

---

# PHASE 9 — UNCERTAINTY

Do not output fake precision.

Estimate prediction uncertainty using an appropriate development-only method.

Possible methods include:

* residual quantiles
* conformal-style intervals compatible with time ordering
* bootstrap/block bootstrap where statistically appropriate

Avoid claims such as:

`Expected return +2.1736%`

if the uncertainty does not justify that precision.

UI-facing outputs can later be rounded.

Research output should retain raw values.

---

# PHASE 10 — MODEL LADDER

Only after the simple models are implemented, create a bounded model ladder.

Suggested order:

M0 — calibrated B3

M1 — B3 + market state

M2 — B3 + market state + only previously validated independent alpha features

M3 — regularized nonlinear model if and only if sample size/power justify it

Potential nonlinear candidate:

* shallow gradient-boosted trees

Do NOT immediately introduce:

* LSTM
* Transformers
* reinforcement learning
* policy gradients
* Q-learning
* neuroevolution

Complexity is not evidence.

Every model must earn the right to advance.

